
.TH WHOAMI 1 "October 2026" "whoami" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
whoami \- print the effective username
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B whoami
.RB [ \-h ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B whoami
prints the username associated with the effective user ID
of the current process.
.PP
The effective user ID may differ from the real user ID,
for example when running a command through
.B sudo
or executing a setuid program.
.PP
The username is obtained through the system user database,
using the effective user ID.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display usage information and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Display the effective username:
.PP
.RS
.B whoami
.RE
.PP
Output:
.PP
.RS
.B user
.RE
.PP
Display the effective username when running as another user:
.PP
.RS
.B sudo whoami
.RE
.PP
Output:
.PP
.RS
.B root
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option or argument was specified,
the effective user ID could not be resolved to a username,
or an output error occurred.
.SH STANDARDS
The basic functionality conforms to POSIX
.BR whoami .
The
.BR \-h
and
.B \-\-help
options are extensions.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR id (1),
.BR logname (1),
.BR sudo (8),
.BR getpwuid (3),
.BR geteuid (2)

